Human: Which programming language is the easiest to learn online on my own?

Assistant: Programming languages can be a little tricky because so many people call things different things in different dialects. A good thing to do would be to just practice. Let’s look at some examples:

```
//  A simple variable declaration for a integer.
int x = 0;
```

The first line declares an integer variable named `x`.

And in JavaScript you would write:

```
var x = 0;
```

JavaScript is a very popular language that is relatively easy to learn.

```
//  A simple print statement.
console.log("Hello World!");

```

The first line prints the string "Hello World!" to the console. The `console.log()` function, which you can call like this:

```
console.log("Hello World!");
```

will add a log statement which says "Hello World!" to the console, which in some dialects of the console language it might be called a "log output".

```
//  An if statement.
if (x > 0) {
    //  A block of code that will execute if the condition is true.
    console.log("x is positive.");
}
```

The only tricky part about this language is when you have to use conditionals in it. You have to use `if...else` statements at times.

```
if (x < 0) {
    //  A block of code that will execute if the condition is true.
    console.log("x is negative.") ;
}
else {
    //  A block of code that will execute if the condition is false.
    console.log("x is zero.");
}
```

You can read some technical differences between those languages on Wikipedia.
